{# JLC BOM step, once per part: the pinned LCSC part is what ships, not the value the circuit asked for. #}
{{ config(each="parts.parts[*]", output_format="json") }}
{% set p = ref('parts') %}
{% set built = ref('board').output.bom | selectattr('ref', 'equalto', p.ref) | list %}
You are reading one line of a JLCPCB assembly BOM before upload.

Part {{ p.ref }} ({{ p.function }}):
{{ p | tojson(indent=2) }}

The line atopile actually generated for {{ p.ref }}:
{{ built[0] | tojson if built else "MISSING: this part is not in the built BOM" }}

Check this one line:
1. Value: does the pinned LCSC part ({{ p.lcsc }}, "{{ p.value }}") match the wanted
   value "{{ p.wanted_value }}"? A design assertion on the declared value passes
   even when the pinned id is 10x off: 51 Ω was wanted, C23193 is 510 Ω.
2. Tier: is it Extended where a Basic part with the same value and package
   exists? Each Extended part adds about $3 to the order.
3. Stock: is this part likely to be low stock or end of life? You cannot see live
   stock, so say "unknown" rather than guess.
4. Package: does the package in the LCSC description match the footprint the
   circuit needs?
5. Assembly: a through-hole or clip-in part (a sensor in terminals, wire pads)
   must be "do_not_place", not quoted for SMT.
6. Built: does the generated line carry the same LCSC id and value as the
   pinned part? A part missing from the built BOM, or built with a different
   id, ships differently from the design.

Return only JSON:
{
  "ref": "{{ p.ref }}",
  "lcsc": "{{ p.lcsc }}",
  "value_matches": true,
  "tier_ok": true,
  "stock": "ok | low | unknown",
  "package_ok": true,
  "built_matches": true,
  "assembly": "smt | do_not_place",
  "issues": ["empty when the line is clean"],
  "verify_on_jlc": "what to compare on JLC's BOM page for this line"
}
